\"}]}\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:3549:{\"workflows\":[{\"id\":2,\"path\":\".github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml\"}]}\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:3588:{\"workflows\":[{\"id\":2,\"path\":\".github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml\"}]}\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:3621:  cat >\"$repo_dir/docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md\" <<'EOF'\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:3644:{\"workflows\":[{\"id\":2,\"path\":\".github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml\"}]}\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:3668:  cat >\"$repo_dir/docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md\" <<'EOF'\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:3690:{\"workflows\":[{\"id\":2,\"path\":\".github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml\"}]}\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:3721:{\"workflows\":[{\"id\":2,\"path\":\".github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml\"}]}\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:3732:  cat >\"$repo_dir/docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md\" <<'EOF'\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:3763:    '~~~' >>\"$repo_dir/docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md\"\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:3764:  run_enable_case \"$repo_dir\" \"$fake_gh_dir\" 2 '' 'was not found in the release-tag SHA cache' --expected-wrapper-sha 2222222222222222222222222222222222222222\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:3774:{\"workflows\":[{\"id\":2,\"path\":\".github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml\"}]}\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:3779:  cat >\"$repo_dir/docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md\" <<'EOF'\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:3796:  run_enable_case \"$repo_dir\" \"$fake_gh_dir\" 2 '' 'cannot be combined with --i-understand-this-repo-has-no-prior-green-ci' --expected-wrapper-sha 1111111111111111111111111111111111111111 --i-understand-this-repo-has-no-prior-green-ci\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:3:**Last Updated:** 2026-05-10 (D-047 filed for WP-SCP-024 024B-extras; restore/break-glass/workflow wiring)\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:48:> **D-047 reservation note (2026-05-10):** D-047 was reserved for WP-SCP-024\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:54:| D-001 | 2026-04-11 | Use Python for the phase 1 CLI-first implementation | ACCEPTED | Fast path to explicit schemas, local tooling, JSON validation, and deterministic file-based execution |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:81:| D-029 | 2026-04-29 | `policy-check.yml` reusable workflow `permissions:` block expands to `contents: read, statuses: write` to support WP-SCP-022 020C.1(vi) read-back commit-status posting on the sibling context `scp/policy-check-readback`. Original 020B(iii) baseline was `contents: read` only. The `statuses: write` privilege uses the caller's `${{ github.token }}` and posts only to `repos/<caller>/statuses/${{ pull_request.head.sha }}`; no other write surface is unlocked. Disabling the read-back would require accepting a regression on 020C.1(vi); keeping `contents: read` only would prevent the read-back from being posted | ACCEPTED | The read-back surface is the only place a PR reviewer sees waiver/disabled-rule posture without reading JSON artefacts; WP-SCP-020 §4.3 fixpoint criteria for 020C.1(vi) require it. The privilege footprint is narrowest possible — `statuses: write` does not allow content modification, branch operations, issue/PR comments, or token impersonation. The workflow checkout uses `persist-credentials: false` so the elevated privilege is bounded to the policy-check job. Plan amendment: `docs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md` §4 020B(iii) updated with cross-reference to D-029. Identified by WP-SCP-022 R1 completeness review (MAJ-005) |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:82:| D-030 | 2026-04-30 | Apply WP-SCP-020 slice 020J on the SCP repository: (a) tag-protection rule on `v*` (legacy tag-protection API and/or Repository Ruleset) preventing force-push or deletion of release tags; (b) `required-signed-commits` on `main` so every commit reaching `main` carries a verified signature. Both are preconditions for slice 020D1 (self-dogfood wrapper pinned at the v1.0.0-rc.1 commit-SHA) and 020D2 (required-status-check + v1.0.0 cut). Configured via `scripts/configure-020j-protections.sh` (idempotent API caller). Documented in `docs/security/branch-protection.md`. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: a single GitHub admin (@jrnb2024) can disable these via repo Settings; quarterly review cadence per WP-SCP-020 §14 U-sec-2 closure | ACCEPTED | Path (a) selected at WP-SCP-020 plan merge per §14 U-sec-2 — Personal GitHub Pro tier supports both protections natively, no signed-release-manifest substitute needed. Tag-protection prevents tag mutation post-cut (the supply-chain anchor for adopters pinning by SHA + comment-tag); required-signed-commits prevents un-attested code from reaching the gate's source of truth. Future direct commits to main without a verified signature are blocked, which is the intent (forces all changes through PRs whose squash-merges are auto-signed by GitHub's bot key). The unsigned commit `bcfc706` (USER-GATE-C signoff, 2026-04-29, unsigned) predates this enablement and is grandfathered. Identified as gate-readiness work in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md` §4 020J + §3 sequence + §8 risks (\"Bootstrap trust\") + §9.1 sequencing |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:85:| D-033 | 2026-04-30 | WP-SCP-022 020D2.1 reconciliation: amend D-032's branch-protection shape on the SCP repo to reflect two operational realities surfaced when the first PR (USER-GATE-A signoff PR #64) attempted to merge through enforced mode. (a) **Required-check context name correction:** WP-SCP-020 020B(xi) specified `scp/policy-check` as the context name. GitHub Actions renders the actual check-run as `<wrapper-workflow-name> / <reusable-job-name>` — for SCP self this is `policy-check / scp/policy-check`. The bare `scp/policy-check` context matches only the readback status posted by the reusable workflow's commit-status step, NOT the primary check-run from the wrapper. Required-check context updated to `policy-check / scp/policy-check`. (b) **Personal-account review-shape correction:** D-032 originally set `required_approving_review_count = 1` + `require_code_owner_reviews = true` per WP-SCP-020 020D2 spec. GitHub forbids PR authors from approving their own PRs. In single-operator mode with @jrnb2024 as the only maintainer AND only CODEOWNER, count=1 + codeowner=true locks the operator out of every PR; `enforce_admins=true` prevents `gh pr merge --admin` from bypassing. Updated to `count=0` + `codeowner_reviews=false` in single-operator mode. CODEOWNERS still serves as documentation of who SHOULD approve when a second maintainer arrives. Forward-looking discipline preserved: when a second maintainer onboards, flip count=1 + codeowner=true + non-author=true in one operation. Both corrections are landed in `scripts/configure-020d2-required-check.sh` (idempotent re-run reproduces the live state) + `docs/security/branch-protection.md` + WP-SCP-020 §8 risk row. The 2026-07-21 quarterly escalation review re-evaluates the personal-account shape | ACCEPTED | First-PR-through-enforcement is the operational test of the spec; the test surfaced two assumptions that didn't survive contact with GitHub's actual behaviour: (1) the bare context name from 020B(xi) was incomplete (didn't anticipate Actions' `<workflow> / <job>` rendering for reusable-workflow callers), and (2) \"self-approval is the only option\" in 020K's wording was operationally impossible (GitHub forbids PR self-review). Both corrections are operational closure of the spec gap; neither weakens the security posture. The 4-layer protection (signed commits + required check + no admin bypass + tag protection) remains intact. The review-count=0 acceptance is the explicit bus-factor-1 cost; review enforcement returns the moment a second maintainer joins. Identified by USER-GATE-A signoff PR #64's failed merge attempt 2026-04-30 |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:87:| D-035 | 2026-04-30 | Adopt the WP-SCP-020 slice 020G adopter-helper invocation procedure as estate doctrine. The required-check context name `policy-check / scp/policy-check` traces to D-033 (the rendered Actions check-run name established for the SCP self-dogfood gate). Adopters of the SCP federation primitive enable this required status check on their default branch via `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` from the SCP repo. The script applies branch protection in two API calls (unified PUT for status-checks/admins/reviews + dedicated POST to `required_signatures` sub-resource per the GitHub API shape), refuses to bypass admin enforcement without an explicit `--i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate` acknowledgement flag, refuses to run in CI (CI=true / GITHUB_ACTIONS=true env-var guards), preserves any pre-existing `required_pull_request_reviews` shape rather than nulling it, and emits an invocation-log block (operator, timestamp, script SHA256 + git SHA, target repo+branch, before/after API JSON) for paste into `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md`. The log commit is part of the invocation procedure: an apply without the log entry is considered unrecorded. Estate cascade rollout (WP-SCP-024) drives this script across PIM, recommender, shopify-app, mapp-doc-agent, control-tower; FLA pilot (WP-SCP-020.1) is the canary first invocation | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-030 (020J `v*` tag-protection), D-031 (020K CODEOWNERS personal-account), D-032 (020D2 SCP-self required-status-check), D-034 (020F `renovate/v*` tag-protection): every operational procedure that mutates third-party repository state ships an idempotent applier + ratifying D-NNN + invocation log convention. The bootstrap-only / no-CI guard reflects that this script writes to a third-party repo's branch protection — an automation tool that ran it unattended could mass-mutate the estate via stolen credentials before any operator noticed. The `--i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate` confirmation reflects that `--no-enforce-admins` defeats the whole point of the federation primitive on the target repo and so should require explicit intent. The `required_pull_request_reviews` preservation reflects that adopters with multi-maintainer teams have legitimate review-shape state that 020G must not destroy. Identified at WP-SCP-020 §4 020G + 020G R1 review (3 MAJ + 5 MIN + 2 nit on safety, 2 MAJ + 4 MIN + nit on completeness, 3 MAJ + 2 MIN + 2 nit on correctness — fixpoint via fix-round-1) |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:95:| D-043 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard exposure surface — markdown report (`docs/scorecards/<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`) + MCP method `scp.consult_scorecard` — per WP-SCP-023 023D (this PR). Markdown generator at `scripts/generate-scorecard-report.py` produces deterministic golden-file-tested output reading `output/scorecards/index.json`; aggregator workflow `.github/workflows/scorecard-aggregator.yml` extended in the commit job to invoke the generator + add the rendered markdown to the operator-review PR alongside the index. MCP method `scp.consult_scorecard` (registered in `src/standards_control_plane/mcp_server/tools.py`): read-only contract per WP-SCP-023 plan-doc invariant 5; request shape `{repo_filter?, since_emitted_at?}`; response shape excludes waiver content (`reason` / `approved_by` / `waiver_id` — invariant 2) by Pydantic model construction (extra=forbid + explicit field whitelist). The MCP method reads `output/scorecards/index.json` from the SCP repo working copy at call time; staleness is observable via the response's `aggregated_at` field. ADOPT-001 §12.7.15 documents the adopter onboarding workflow. NEVER carries waiver content (invariant 2 enforced by Pydantic models + schema + dedicated pytest scans on both markdown + MCP responses) | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-041 + D-042 — D-043 ratifies the CONSUMER side: markdown for operators/auditors, MCP for agents/tooling. Read-only contract + Pydantic-enforced field whitelist + golden-file pytest together preserve invariant 2 across both surfaces. The MCP method does NOT introduce a new authentication primitive — it rides on WP-SCP-021's existing Ed25519 receipt mechanism (consult-class semantics). The markdown report is informational and CODEOWNERS-protected via `docs/scorecards/** @jrnb2024`; operators can hand-edit before merging the aggregator PR if needed (regenerator overwrites on next run). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: the MCP server runs at acc.brokapps.ai; if down, agents lose `consult_scorecard` access but the markdown report + git index remain consultable. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-043 reservation |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:96:| D-044 | 2026-05-09 | Adopt the WP-SCP-024 slice 024B-core operational contracts for cascade scaffolding. Components: (1) `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` (`SCP-073-scaffolder` per WP-SCP-020 §11 follow-up + WP-SCP-021 follow-up `SCP-075-scaffolder-compose`) generates adopter-side artefacts mechanically - bootstrap-only (refuses `CI=true` / `GITHUB_ACTIONS=true` per D-035 symmetry); validates `--scp-sha` against SCP main HEAD lineage (warns if not main HEAD; refuses if non-existent); emits a TF-023E-002 warning for post-v1.2.0 SHAs while recommending the v1.0.0 pin; `--scp-sha` 40-char hex regex; `--default-branch` enum {main, master, develop}; `--scorecard-emit` enum; `--output-dir` writability + path-normalised system-path denylist (incl `/private/var/*` macOS canonicalisation); emits `MANIFEST.json` with SHA256 audit; arguments per plan-doc §5.3. Canonical adopter slugs are normalization aids only, not collision-resistant IDs, so cohort repo names must remain unique after canonicalization. (2) `templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl` is the canonical adopter wrapper shape per ADOPT-001 §12 with Renovate inline-comment marker `# renovate: datasource=github-tags depName=jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-` enabling auto-bump per plan-doc invariant 8 Threshold A criterion; substitutions `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`, `{{SCP_SHA}}`, `{{SCORECARD_EMIT}}`; least-privilege workflow-level permissions (`contents:read` + `statuses:write` only). (3) `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh` CLI tool implements plan-doc invariant 2 cascade-status CI enforcement: 4 enforcement behaviours (fail-closed default for absent/unrecognised; `onboarded` -> log-entry-modified + target-match; `onboarded-operator-bump` -> log entry + `STATUS.md` `TF-024X-renovate-<slug>` row matching invariant 2 regex literally; `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` -> `DISPATCH-NOTE` `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` reference + log file NOT modified) + explicit tooling-slice opt-in gate for `cascade-status: not applicable` (`--allow-not-applicable`; default refusal for direct CLI use). Regex via `python3 re.search()` (PCRE-style); exactly-one-match assertions on both `cascade-status` and `**Target:**` extraction close multi-occurrence injection bypasses; adopter-slug canonicalisation per plan-doc spec. CLI tool only - GitHub Actions workflow wiring (`.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml`) defers to slice 024B-extras alongside `enable-required-check.sh --restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass procedure. D-044 was reserved for slice 024B at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge (PR #102, 2026-05-04); after the operator-decided scope-split (SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md), D-044 stays scoped to core deliverables; sibling D-NNN may be reserved at 024B-extras authoring time for the deferred surface (`--restore` + break-glass + workflow wiring). Slice 024B-core ships under documented protocol deviation per feedback_four_tier_dispatch.md note + justify rule (kernel hook lives in ACC repo only; per-repo install pattern is FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 - open). | ACCEPTED | D-044 was reserved at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge per the §9 reservation block. The original 024B accumulated 7 fix-rounds + 7 R-rounds against a single branch with each round closing 5-6 MAJ but introducing 2-3 new defects in just-added code (R6 missed primary-deliverable workflow wiring; R7 fix introduced bootstrap defect on slice's own PR). Empirical signal: surface area too large (16 files, ~3500 lines added) for single-pass convergence. Operator decided 2026-05-09 to split 024B into 024B-core (this slice; ~5 files; scaffolder + template + CI enforcement script + tests) + 024B-extras (deferred; `--restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass + `.github/workflows/` CI wiring + step 7 operator-interactive demo). Split is incremental delivery, NOT descope - both halves still ship; both required before 024C cohort onboarding opens. Original 024B work preserved at `feature/wp-scp-024-024b-extras-parking` branch (audit trail: 7 codex-fix-round-N-audit.log files + 7 R-round archive directories incl r8-archive carry-forward findings). Slice 024B-core scope is internally coherent: scaffolder generates adopter artefacts; CI enforcement script (CLI) validates cascade-status declarations; tests cover both. The CLI script can be invoked manually as a stop-gap until 024B-extras wires it into GitHub Actions. The CLI guard is necessary-but-not-sufficient: workflow-side hardcoding of `--tooling-slice-id` and omission of `--allow-not-applicable` in 024B-extras is the canonical closure path for the operator-supplied not-applicable bypass surface (see FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc §9 D-044 reservation |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:97:| D-047 | 2026-05-10 | Adopt WP-SCP-024 024B-extras operational contracts: `scripts/enable-required-check.sh --restore <pre-state.json>` restores branch protection from the captured prior-state log entry by transforming GET-shape JSON into the PUT body, stripping envelope fields recursively, converting `enforce_admins.enabled` to a boolean, and replaying `required_signatures` through the dedicated sub-resource; restore-mode posture-degradation flags `--i-understand-restore-removes-admin-enforcement`, `--i-understand-restore-removes-required-checks`, `--i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes`, and `--i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions` are mandatory when the target removes or re-enables those protections; forward-mode now performs a path-pinned workflow lookup for `.github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml`, queries workflow-runs without a branch filter, validates `--expected-wrapper-sha` against actual release tags via `gh api repos/jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-/git/ref/tags/<tag>`, and treats the working-tree diff of `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` as part of prior-entry detection; the new CI workflow `.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml` wires the CLI gate into PR checks with `paths: docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[C-Z]*/**` plus explicit branch-protection-log.md / STATUS.md triggers and tooling-slice short-circuiting; ADOPT-001 gains §12.8 break-glass procedure and explicitly names the `--i-understand-no-gate-2-verification` escape hatch; STATUS.md and the DISPATCH-NOTE placeholder for the step-7 operator demo are updated in-slice. | ACCEPTED | The 024B split left the restore / break-glass / workflow-integration surface as the deferred half so the review load stayed bounded. This decision ratifies the operational contract that lets operators disable and re-enable the federation primitive safely without widening the adopter-facing API surface, while preserving the bootstrap guard that prevents tooling slices from self-blocking their own PRs. The release-tag SHA requirement is intentionally anchored to actual git tags, not arbitrary commits, so Gate 3 can re-arm against a concrete release artifact rather than against moving main-head. After any break-glass cycle, `prior_restore_evidence_present` scans the full git history, so every subsequent forward-mode re-enable for the same adopter permanently requires `--expected-wrapper-sha` unless the operator deliberately takes the audited Gate 2 bypass; that permanence is intentional. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: the helper remains operator-run, the escape hatch is explicitly auditable, and the restore demo evidence is still an operator-interactive step outside Codex dispatch. Identified at WP-SCP-024 024B-extras authoring time. |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:99:> D-044 limitation note: the CLI guard is necessary-but-not-sufficient. 024B-extras must hardcode `--tooling-slice-id` per workflow file and omit `--allow-not-applicable` from cohort workflows; that workflow-side hardcoding is the canonical closure path for the operator-supplied not-applicable bypass surface (see FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
